WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.400
The p99 that pages you at three a m.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.362
The average looks fine, yet users time out.

3
00:00:06.362 --> 00:00:07.600
Let's find the tail.

4
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.800
Your chat API has a comfortable median latency of nine hundred milliseconds.

5
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.833
But p ninety nine is forty seven seconds, in bursts at peak.

6
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:19.933
And adding replicas didn't help.

7
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:22.000
Here's why.

8
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.567
The bottleneck isn't your app's CPU.

9
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:26.833
More replicas just add more clients.

10
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:31.433
They all line up behind the same provider limit, connection pool, or batch queue.

11
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:33.667
Sometimes the waits get even longer.

12
00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.167
A bimodal shape has several suspects.

13
00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:40.167
Rate limit errors with synchronized retries.

14
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:42.833
One blocking call stalling the event loop.

15
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:44.800
Exhausted connection pools.

16
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.400
A few huge prompts hogging capacity.

17
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.567
Or autoscaling that's too slow.

18
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:53.000
To find which one, look at your traces.

19
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.367
Compare the spans of a median request with a p ninety nine request.

20
00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.833
Whichever span explodes is your culprit.

21
00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:02.767
It takes ten minutes, and beats hours of theory.

22
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:05.633
Then match the fix to the cause.

23
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:08.367
A semaphore keeps you under provider limits.

24
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:10.567
Exponential backoff with jitter.

25
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:15.333
A queue with a timeout, so a doomed request fails gracefully at five seconds.

26
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:18.200
And move non essential work off the hot path.

27
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.300
The trap is optimizing the mean.

28
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.422
Monitor percentiles separately, plus queue wait, rate limit errors, and time to first token.

29
00:01:27.422 --> 00:01:29.700
Tail problems are queueing problems.

30
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:31.692
Let's recap.

31
00:01:31.692 --> 00:01:34.267
Replicas can't fix a shared bottleneck.

32
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:36.667
Compare median and tail traces.

33
00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:39.133
Use semaphores, jitter, and timeouts.

34
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:41.696
Watch percentiles, not averages.

35
00:01:41.696 --> 00:01:44.667
Next up: the eval everyone disagrees with.
